{"reference":"Ruth 3","book":"Ruth","chapter":3,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the threshing-floor scene","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The threshing-floor scene contains risk and suggestive language, but the narrative presents Ruth’s request to Boaz as an appeal for redemption, and Boaz responds by praising her character. The text does not describe a completed sexual encounter; that conclusion goes beyond what it says. Across the book, the LORD is credited with providing food and conception, while the story itself advances through the actions of Naomi, Ruth, Boaz, and others.","verse_refs":["Ruth 3:1-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_003_0d5b9801553e282a74cd31ac"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The wider story","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Within Ruth’s larger movement, the story travels from famine, bereavement, and Naomi’s sense of emptiness toward harvest, kinship redemption, birth, communal blessing, and genealogy. Ruth 3 advances that movement by bringing Ruth’s request and Boaz’s promise toward a public resolution.","verse_refs":["Ruth 1","Ruth 2:1-23","Ruth 4:1-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_003_e9796585b8ba293c08ece1f1"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"},{"id":"block_3","text":"A later passage in Nehemiah applies Deuteronomy’s exclusion of Ammonites and Moabites in a temple-related setting. Read alongside Ruth, whose central figure is a Moabite, this material should not be reduced to a universal ethnic ban; the wider biblical witness also includes welcome for faithful foreigners.","verse_refs":["Ruth 1:1-22","Ruth 2:1-23","Ruth 4:1-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_003_522201dded45cbf69c08b804"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}